{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_1","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc 'pwd; nvidia-smi --query-gpu=name,memory.total,memory.free --format=csv 2>/dev/null || echo NO_GPU; ls -a; cat STATE.md; cat literature_review.md; cat resources.md'","aggregated_output":"/workspaces/is-there-a-sounds-like-ai-direction-c05a-codex-gpt-6-astra\nname, memory.total [MiB], memory.free [MiB]\nNVIDIA RTX A6000, 49140 MiB, 48541 MiB\n.\n..\n.claude\n.codex\n.gemini\n.git\n.gitignore\n.neurico\n.resource_finder_complete\n.venv\nREADME.md\nSTATE.md\nartifacts\ncode\ndatasets\nliterature_review.md\nlogs\npaper_search_results\npapers\nplanning.md\npyproject.toml\nresources.md\nresults\nscripts\nuv.lock\n# Research State\n\n- Current phase: `experiment_runner`\n- Pipeline completed: `False`\n\n## Previous phases\n\nresource_finder (succeeded)\n\n## Current phase context\n\n- Phase: `experiment_runner`\n- Status: `in_progress`\n- Started: `2026-10-04T10:05:37.281491Z`\n- Next steps:\n  - Validate the report and experimental artifacts before finalizing.\n\n## Workspace check\n\n- Root: `/workspaces/is-there-a-sounds-like-ai-direction-c05a-codex-gpt-6-astra`\n- Directory usable: `True`\n\n## Output validation\n\nNo phase output validation recorded yet.\n\n## Agent notes\n\n<!-- NEURICO_AGENT_NOTES_START -->\n### resource_finder\n<!-- NEURICO_AGENT_NOTES_START:resource_finder -->\nResource-finder Phases 1–5 complete. Required artifacts: literature_review.md, resources.md, planning.md, papers/datasets/code READMEs, reproducibility scripts, and .resource_finder_complete. Final integrity audit passed: artifacts/resource_finder/final_audit.json.\nInventory: 10 source PDFs (all 43 chunks/127 pages of four specified core papers read; notes in artifacts/resource_finder/reading_log.md); four benchmarks in 15 raw files, 1,543,038,962 bytes (HC3 English full, HAP-E full, MAGE all CSVs, RAID clean train subset); 519 auxiliary persona prompt files; six pinned sparse repos. Manifests record SHA256/revisions; raw resources excluded from Git. Fresh .venv and uv.lock ready; 24 resource-environment packages consistent. Upstream 198 Python files syntax-checked; no model runtime tests or weights downloaded.\nRanking fixed in planning.md: (1) signed causal dose response, (2) nuisance/persona distinctness, (3) cross-domain/generator/model transfer; five alternatives pruned with reasons. Readout is not causality; prior SAE steering can change facts. Dataset cautions: HAP-E context vs comparator; HC3 missing/duplicate questions; MAGE human=1 and 52 valid/test exact overlaps; RAID imbalance/source grouping. SV-Detect code/docs mismatch and pair centering; Fast-DetectGPT needs fresh calibration. Paper-finder fast retry returned zero; manual primary-source search supplied all resources.\nNext: experiment_runner should read literature_review.md and planning.md, choose compatible model dependencies in this project environment, load grouped/deduplicated splits, freeze independent detectors, and run the small causal pilot before scaling the three directions. RTX A6000 available; Qwen3-1.7B candidate verified. Near-duplicate leakage, hook-coordinate fidelity, nuisance coverage, model runtime compatibility, and numerical reproduction remain explicit experiment-stage checks. No scientific outcomes claimed; generated pipeline sections unchanged.\n<!-- NEURICO_AGENT_NOTES_END:resource_finder -->\n\n### experiment_runner\n<!-- NEURICO_AGENT_NOTES_START:experiment_runner -->\nUpdate this section at the end of the `experiment_runner` phase.\n<!-- NEURICO_AGENT_NOTES_END:experiment_runner -->\n\n<!-- NEURICO_AGENT_NOTES_END -->\n# Is there a “sounds like AI” direction in the residual stream?\n\n## Scope and conclusion\n\nThere is strong evidence that human/AI text provenance is linearly readable from frozen LLM representations, and prior work causally steers individual stylistic features. The reviewed evidence does **not establish a single, distinct, content-preserving AI-style generation direction**. The useful contribution is therefore a controlled intervention with independent detectors and explicit nuisance tests, rather than another separability result.\n\nScope: 2023–2026 work on representation-based detection, causal residual steering, persona geometry, and matched human/AI corpora. Include direct methodological evidence and reusable resources; exclude unrelated watermarking, general-purpose fine-tuning, and detector-evasion optimization. This is a focused resource review, not an exhaustive systematic review. All four specified references were read through all 43 three-page-or-shorter chunks (127 pages); six supplemental papers were screened by abstract/first chunk and selected methods/results. Detailed per-chunk evidence is in [reading_log.md](artifacts/resource_finder/reading_log.md); bibliographic details and PDFs in [papers/README.md](papers/README.md).\n\n## Search and screening\n\nSearch date: 2026-10-04. Keywords: human/AI residual representations; machine-generated text linear probes; activation steering; contrastive activation addition; assistant persona axis; SAE style features; matched continuations; domain/generator transfer; independent detection; style/content disentanglement.\n\n|Search|Source|Outcome|\n|---|---|---|\n|Human/AI text residual probing and steering, diligent|Paper-finder|Initial missing HTTP dependency repaired; subsequent run terminated without results.|\n|AI text detection activation steering, fast retry|Paper-finder|Success with zero papers; [log](artifacts/resource_finder/paper_finder_retry.log).|\n|Four specified URLs and their method/code references|arXiv, Anthropic|All resolved/downloaded; four included for full reading.|\n|CAA, RAID/MAGE, HC3, HAP-E, Fast-DetectGPT|arXiv, ACL Anthology, official HF/GitHub|Six supporting PDFs included; official implementations/data inspected.|\n|ActAdd, FGAA, broad detection surveys|Search screening|Background only; CAA and supplied SAE paper cover required intervention baselines. No fourth direction added.|\n\n## Key papers\n\n### 1. Linear Probing Provides Robust and Efficient Detection of Machine-Generated Text\n\n**Quaremba, Yan, Black, Vrandečić and Simperl, 2026**, [arXiv:2608.24780](https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.24780), v1; [code](https://github.com/gerritq/mgt_probes). Last-token residuals, 100-component PCA per layer, and logistic probes form layer-averaged (LLP) or concatenated-layer (CLP) detectors. Detection uses DetectRL, MultiSocial, RAID and TSM, 16 settings with 1,500 balanced training and 500 test examples each; M4GT supports geometry analyses. Sixteen baselines include RoBERTa, RepreGuard, TextFluoroscopy, Fast-DetectGPT and Binoculars.\n\nReported mean OOD improvements range 0.39–11.37 AUROC points. Probe alignment and correlations with editing strength support shared readout structure, not generation causality. The low-label PCA experiment observes the full training set, although a no-PCA appendix supports similar sample efficiency. Authors explicitly leave universality/confounding/causality unresolved. Implementation standardizes before PCA: inverse-map the classifier normal into residual coordinates before intervention.\n\n### 2. SV-Detect: AI-generated Text Detection with Steering Vectors\n\n**Vishnyakov and Gaintseva, 2026**, [arXiv:2606.07313](https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.07313), v2; [code](https://github.com/Atmyre/sv-detect). Frozen GPT-Neo-2.7B mean-pooled states yield per-layer mean-difference, logistic, or paired-PCA directions; cosine features feed a standardized logistic head. DetectRL, MIRAGE and PADBen evaluate domain/generator/editing transfer; RAID/COLING extend evaluation. Metrics include AUROC, AUPR, F1, MCC and TPR at fixed FPR; baselines include score-based detectors, RoBERTa variants and RepreGuard.\n\nDirections are explicitly used **only for probing/classification**. Logistic directions outperform mean/PCA in difficult transfer; regex typography/discourse/lexical baselines leave additional signal unexplained. This does not identify its causal meaning. RAID transfer remains much weaker for non-chat generators; a multi-direction variant reports 56.3% overall TPR at 5% FPR. Near-perfect RAID matched-training results are a different protocol. Code/documentation disagree on vector C and head intercept; [code notes](code/README.md) record exact discrepancies.\n\n### 3. Feature-Level Insights into Artificial Text Detection with Sparse Autoencoders\n\n**Kuznetsov et al., 2025**, [arXiv:2503.03601](https://arxiv.org/abs/2503.03601), v1; [feature explorer](https://mgtsaevis.github.io/mgt-sae-visualization/). Gemma-2-2B/Gemma Scope residual SAE features are summed over tokens and classified with XGBoost; baselines are raw activation XGBoost, individual-feature thresholds and a shared-task multi-task detector. COLING-2025 supplies train/dev/devtest/test and RAID supplies attack analysis. Layer 16 generalizes well by macro-F1, but individual features vary across domains/generators.\n\nCrucially, the paper already steers decoder directions, scaled by reference maximum activation, and interprets 50-generation sets with GPT-4o. Features affect complexity, assertion, verbosity, repetition and formality. Appendix examples show factual corruption at strong doses. The judge interprets features rather than independently measuring detector score and preserved facts. One model/SAE, summed-feature length dependence, source artifacts and all-token intervention limit causal generalization. This is the closest prior intervention work and prevents claiming first AI-style feature steering.\n\n### 4. The Assistant Axis: Situating and Stabilizing the Default Persona of Language Models\n\n**Lu, Gallagher, Michala, Fish and Lindsey, 2026**, [arXiv:2601.10387](https://arxiv.org/abs/2601.10387), v1; [specified article](https://www.anthropic.com/research/assistant-axis), [code](https://github.com/safety-research/assistant-axis). Gemma2-27B, Qwen3-32B and Llama3.3-70B response activations span 275 roles and 240 traits. The assistant-minus-role mean aligns with role-space PC1. Addition changes role adoption; projection capping reduces persona-jailbreak harmful responses by roughly 60% in selected configurations while preserving measured IFEval/MMLU-Pro/GSM8K/EQ-Bench performance.\n\nThese are persona/safety outcomes, not AI-writing detection. Synthetic conversations, qualitative persona labels, large-model scope and implementation differences constrain conclusions. Assistant projection is therefore a necessary nuisance control, estimated in the same model/layer as AI-style vectors. Do not transfer published vectors directly to another model, or equate capping, ablation and signed addition.\n\n### 5. Steering Llama 2 via Contrastive Activation Addition\n\n**Rimsky et al., ACL 2024**, [published paper](https://aclanthology.org/2024.acl-long.828/), local `2312.06681.pdf`; [code](https://github.com/nrimsky/CAA). Paired positive/negative answer-letter residual differences are averaged and added with signed coefficients after the prompt. Llama2-7B/13B evaluations cover seven behaviors using multiple-choice and open generation, GPT-4 judging, and capability controls; system prompting and fine-tuning are comparators. It supplies the causal intervention recipe, not evidence about provenance style. Prompt-format dependence and collateral changes motivate random-vector and quality controls.\n\n### 6. Do LLMs write like humans? Variation in grammatical and rhetorical styles\n\n**Reinhart et al., 2024/2025**, [arXiv:2410.16107](https://arxiv.org/abs/2410.16107), v2. HAP-E and CAP compare human continuations with six model continuations from the same context. Biber's 66 linguistic features support random forest/logistic classifiers and paired Wilcoxon tests with multiplicity correction. Seven-source HAP-E classification reaches 66% versus 14% chance; this is not binary detector accuracy. Instruction models show stronger stylistic deviations than base models; cross-model/domain transfer is imperfect. This supports domain/formality/base-versus-chat controls. HAP-E has 8,290 common sources after refusal/incomplete-output filtering; context matching does not equate factual content. Public corpus and linguistic tooling are linked in the paper/card; no separate implementation cloned.\n\n### 7–10. Benchmarks and independent scoring\n\n|Paper|Method, data and evidence|Use and limitations|\n|---|---|---|\n|**Dugan et al., RAID, ACL 2024**, [2405.07940](https://arxiv.org/abs/2405.07940), [code](https://github.com/liamdugan/raid)|Original 6M+ generations, 11 generators, 8 domains, 4 decoding strategies, 11 attacks; compares 8 open and 4 closed detectors at low FPR.|Decoding/generator/attack shifts undermine detector robustness. Current corpus is larger; downloaded clean train is only a documented subset.|\n|**Guo et al., HC3, 2023**, [2301.07597](https://arxiv.org/abs/2301.07597), [code/models](https://github.com/Hello-SimpleAI/chatgpt-comparison-detection)|Human/ChatGPT answers to shared questions; GLTR logistic, RoBERTa single-text and QA detectors; F1 across full/filtered/sentence variants.|Useful question matching; old single-generator and domain/format artifacts constrain inference. English release has 24,322 question records.|\n|**Li et al., MAGE, ACL 2024**, [2305.13242](https://arxiv.org/abs/2305.13242), [code](https://github.com/yafuly/MAGE)|27 LLMs, 7 families, 10 source corpora, 8 testbeds; Longformer, GLTR/FastText, DetectGPT and human/ChatGPT judgments.|OOD human false positives can be severe; small target-domain adaptation changes the protocol. Do not conflate adapted with zero-shot results.|\n|**Bao et al., Fast-DetectGPT, ICLR 2024**, [2310.05130](https://arxiv.org/abs/2310.05130), [code](https://github.com/baoguangsheng/fast-detect-gpt)|Conditional log-likelihood discrepancy with analytic variance; XSum/SQuAD/WritingPrompts plus domain/language shifts; likelihood/rank/entropy/DetectGPT baselines. Reports 0.9887 white-box and 0.9338 ChatGPT/GPT4 black-box mean AUROC in its setup.|Useful frozen external criterion, not an infallible authorship score. Reported 340x speedup is setup-specific. Recalibrate demo probabilities; ensure tokenizer/reference/scorer compatibility.|\n\n## Synthesis: methods, baselines and measurement\n\nThe common pipeline is frozen activations → pooled representation → learned direction → detector score. Generation intervention adds a separate causal step and can fail even with excellent readout. A classifier normal, mean difference, PCA component and SAE decoder column solve different problems; none is automatically the optimal causal control vector. Multi-layer detection scores are not single-layer vectors.\n\nMinimum baselines: unsteered model; signed mean-difference and logistic-normal interventions; label-shuffled and norm-matched random vectors; formal/informal and concise/verbose prompts; independently estimated assistant/formality/length/domain directions. SAE interpretations inform these controls without adding a fourth experiment. Same-detector improvements alone can reflect detector-specific exploitation.\n\nReport readout AUROC/AUPR and TPR at validation-calibrated FPR. Primary generation outcomes are **paired external score changes jointly with factual retention and coherence**. Add instruction adherence, repetition, output lengths, truncation and detector disagreement. Blinded ratings and source-grounded QA complement semantic similarity; embedding similarity alone cannot establish preserved facts. Use source-cluster bootstrap confidence intervals, seed-paired comparisons, and frozen validation-selected layer/scale settings. Show full score–quality curves rather than discarding incoherent failures.\n\n## Recommendations and unresolved questions\n\nImplement only the three ranked directions in [planning.md](planning.md): (1) signed causal dose response, (2) nuisance/persona separation, (3) held-out domain/generator and second-model transfer. HAP-E is primary, HC3 adds task shift, RAID/MAGE stress generalization. All requested data are local; [dataset notes](datasets/README.md) include label conventions and validated overlaps. An RTX A6000 is available; a small accessible model is a practical first pilot. Model weights and experiment dependencies are not installed.\n\nSplit by source before fitting PCA, scalers, directions, nuisance bases or detectors. HAP-E human chunk 1 is context only; HC3 indices are nonunique; MAGE labels are inverted relative to AI=1 and its validation/test files overlap; RAID variants share source IDs. Published provenance labels and frozen detectors operationalize style but do not define a universal essence of AI writing.\n\nA distinct causal-style claim requires effects surviving independent scoring, factual/coherence constraints, nuisance interventions and transfer. A null or narrower result—readout-only, persona-mediated, detector-specific or quality-damaging—is scientifically useful. Orthogonalization alone is not proof of semantic independence, and removing a nuisance subspace can remove genuine style signal. No causal outcome has been measured in this resource phase.\n# Resources catalog\n\nPrepared 2026-10-04 for **“Is there a ‘sounds like AI’ direction in the residual stream?”** Resource gathering is complete; no experiment results are claimed. Start with [literature_review.md](literature_review.md), then [planning.md](planning.md), which fixes the implementation budget at **three directions**.\n\n## Inventory\n\n- **10 source PDFs**, plus reading chunks/manifests. All four specified references available and fully read.\n- **4 requested benchmark datasets**: complete HC3 English, complete requested HAP-E, all MAGE CSVs, and RAID's complete clean training partition (**subset of full RAID**). Fifteen raw files, **1,543,038,962 bytes** total.\n- **1 auxiliary prompt pack**: 519 assistant-axis JSON/JSONL files, 2,653,728 bytes.\n- **6 official code repositories**, pinned shallow/sparse clones with code and docs.\n- Fresh project `.venv`, `pyproject.toml`, `uv.lock`; download/data/PDF tools installed locally. No model weights or experimental ML stack installed.\n\n## Papers\n\n|Title / authors / year|Local PDF|Use|\n|---|---|---|\n|Linear Probing Provides Robust and Efficient Detection of Machine-Generated Text — Quaremba et al., 2026|[2608.24780](papers/2608.24780.pdf)|Last-token linear readout; causal gap.|\n|SV-Detect: AI-generated Text Detection with Steering Vectors — Vishnyakov & Gaintseva, 2026|[2606.07313](papers/2606.07313.pdf)|Layerwise direction detector, transfer and surface-style baseline.|\n|Feature-Level Insights into Artificial Text Detection with Sparse Autoencoders — Kuznetsov et al., 2025|[2503.03601](papers/2503.03601.pdf)|Existing causal style-feature steering and factual damage.|\n|The Assistant Axis — Lu et al., 2026|[2601.10387](papers/2601.10387.pdf)|Assistant-persona confound/control; paper linked by specified Anthropic article.|\n|Steering Llama 2 via Contrastive Activation Addition — Rimsky et al., ACL 2024|[2312.06681](papers/2312.06681.pdf)|Paired difference and signed residual intervention.|\n|RAID — Dugan et al., ACL 2024|[2405.07940](papers/2405.07940.pdf)|Generator/domain/decoding robustness.|\n|How Close is ChatGPT to Human Experts? — Guo et al., 2023|[2301.07597](papers/2301.07597.pdf)|HC3 corpus and detector baselines.|\n|MAGE: Machine-generated Text Detection in the Wild — Li et al., ACL 2024|[2305.13242](papers/2305.13242.pdf)|Heterogeneous detection and OOD testbeds.|\n|Do LLMs write like humans? — Reinhart et al., 2024/2025|[2410.16107](papers/2410.16107.pdf)|Matched continuations, grammar/rhetoric and instruction-tuning effects.|\n|Fast-DetectGPT — Bao et al., ICLR 2024|[2310.05130](papers/2310.05130.pdf)|External conditional-curvature scoring.|\n\nFull titles/authors/version details: [papers/README.md](papers/README.md). Source URLs, byte counts, page counts and SHA256: [paper_manifest.json](artifacts/resource_finder/paper_manifest.json). All 43 chunks of the four core papers were visually read; notes: [reading_log.md](artifacts/resource_finder/reading_log.md). Supplementary text extractions and rendered sheets are local ignored working artifacts, not substitutes for the PDFs.\n\nRecreate papers from versioned URLs:\n\n```bash\npwd\nuv sync\n.venv/bin/python scripts/download_papers.py\n```\n\n## Datasets\n\n|Dataset / source|Local size and records|Task / location|Important constraint|\n|---|---|---|---|\n|[HC3](https://huggingface.co/datasets/Hello-SimpleAI/HC3)|73.7MB; 24,322 question records; 58,546 human and 26,903 AI answers|QA comparison; `datasets/hc3/all.jsonl`|455 missing AI lists, duplicate questions and nonunique indices; English only.|\n|[HAP-E](https://huggingface.co/datasets/browndw/human-ai-parallel-corpus)|114.1MB; 8,290 shared sources, 66,320 chunks|Primary context-matched continuations; `datasets/hape/`|Chunk1=context; chunk2=human comparator; join ID before `@`. Six generators.|\n|[MAGE](https://huggingface.co/datasets/yaful/MAGE)|553.6MB; 436,606 rows across 5 files|Broad detector training/OOD evaluation; `datasets/mage/`|Raw human=1, AI=0. 52 valid/test text overlaps; OOD tests share 762 human controls.|\n|[RAID](https://huggingface.co/datasets/liamdugan/raid)|801.7MB; 467,985 clean-train rows; 13,371 source groups|Domain/generator/decoding shift; `datasets/raid/train_none.csv`|Authors' official clean mirror; no attacks/extra/test downloaded. Group by source_id and balance labels.|\n|[Assistant-axis prompts](https://github.com/safety-research/assistant-axis)|2.65MB; 519 parsed JSON/JSONL files|Auxiliary persona/nuisance extraction; `datasets/assistant_axis_prompts/`|No pretrained vectors, no detector labels; recompute on target model.|\n\nDecimal MB. All raw data excluded by `datasets/.gitignore`; documentation and excerpts below 100KB/file are retained. [datasets/README.md](datasets/README.md) has exact download/load instructions, licenses, schemas, source revisions, counts and preprocessing warnings. [Validation report](artifacts/resource_finder/dataset_validation.json) checks entire files, not just a prefix. [Download manifest](artifacts/resource_finder/dataset_downloads.json) and [prompt manifest](artifacts/resource_finder/assistant_prompt_manifest.json) provide checksums.\n\n```bash\npwd\n.venv/bin/python scripts/download_datasets.py\n.venv/bin/python scripts/validate_datasets.py\n```\n\n## Code repositories\n\n|Repository|Local checkout|Reuse|\n|---|---|---|\n|[gerritq/mgt_probes](https://github.com/gerritq/mgt_probes)|`code/mgt_probes/`|LLP/CLP training and activation extraction.|\n|[Atmyre/sv-detect](https://github.com/Atmyre/sv-detect)|`code/sv-detect/`|Direction variants, cosine features, logistic detector, regex confounds.|\n|[safety-research/assistant-axis](https://github.com/safety-research/assistant-axis)|`code/assistant-axis/`|Addition/ablation/capping hooks and persona-axis construction.|\n|[nrimsky/CAA](https://github.com/nrimsky/CAA)|`code/CAA/`|Paired difference and generation steering reference.|\n|[baoguangsheng/fast-detect-gpt](https://github.com/baoguangsheng/fast-detect-gpt)|`code/fast-detect-gpt/`|Independent analytic curvature criterion.|\n|[liamdugan/raid](https://github.com/liamdugan/raid)|`code/raid/`|Detection evaluation, subgroup metrics, decoding/attack references.|\n\nNo explicit code_references were supplied; these are official repositories discovered from the specified papers and selected baselines. See [code/README.md](code/README.md) for full commit IDs, dependencies, exact functions, implementation caveats and validation limits. [code_manifest.json](artifacts/resource_finder/code_manifest.json) records selected directories and syntax checks. Sparse checkouts deliberately omit large upstream results/data; six READMEs and critical functions were inspected. All 198 checked-out Python files parsed successfully; **no inference/runtime test was performed**.\n\n```bash\npwd\n.venv/bin/python scripts/clone_resources.py\n.venv/bin/python scripts/extract_assistant_prompts.py\n```\n\n## Model and compute handoff\n\nAvailable hardware: one NVIDIA RTX A6000, 49,140MiB total memory reported by `nvidia-smi`. Candidate metadata/revisions are recorded in [model_candidates.json](artifacts/resource_finder/model_candidates.json); **weights are not downloaded**.\n\n- [Qwen3-1.7B](https://huggingface.co/Qwen/Qwen3-1.7B): accessible small pilot, Apache-2.0; use `enable_thinking=False` explicitly in chat formatting and a compatible Transformers version (card requires >=4.51). Fix decoding settings across all conditions.\n- [Gemma-2-2B-it](https://huggingface.co/google/gemma-2-2b-it): possible second model; gated terms/access required. The SAE paper's base-model feature IDs are not automatically valid for this instruction checkpoint.\n- [GPT-Neo-2.7B](https://huggingface.co/EleutherAI/gpt-neo-2.7B): ungated reference for SV-Detect/Fast-DetectGPT; keep scoring model distinct from the steered generator.\n\nInstall one compatible experimental stack with project-root `uv add`, not the mutually incompatible upstream requirements combined. Hook behavior, masks, final-norm indexing, dtype, KV-cache handling and exported PCA directions need numerical checks in the experiment phase.\n\n## Gathering notes and remaining limits\n\nSearch used paper-finder first; repaired missing HTTP dependency, then used official arXiv/ACL/Anthropic and linked GitHub/HF sources after a zero-result retry. No relevant papers were returned by that successful finder query. Ten selected PDFs include every specified reference and six method/dataset supports. This is a focused review; novelty beyond this literature set remains uncertain.\n\nThe main implementation findings are SV-Detect README/YAML versus source C/intercept discrepancies, per-pair centering, non-guarded zero norms, and Fast-DetectGPT's non-universal demo calibration. Preserve them as explicit adaptation decisions. Old CAA/Fast-DetectGPT and newer SV-Detect dependency pins conflict; model execution is intentionally deferred to experiment setup.\n\nRAID's large attacked corpus was handled using the authors' complete clean partition, not an arbitrary initial sample. All other requested benchmark release files are present. Normalized exact HC3/MAGE overlap was zero, but near-duplicates/common source documents remain to audit. HAP-E controls context, not exact propositions; independent content-constrained tasks are required for the strongest causal claim.\n\n## Recommended experiments\n\n1. **Causal dose response:** HAP-E/HC3-derived direction, signed interventions, zero/random/shuffled-label and style-prompt controls. Score held-out generations with frozen independent detectors and joint content/coherence constraints.\n2. **Distinctness:** matched formality/verbosity/domain/persona vectors, residualized directions, nuisance-only interventions and crossed prompt conditions. Compare in the same model/layer coordinates.\n3. **Transfer:** held-out domains/generators and a separately estimated second-model direction; use RAID/MAGE stress tests, retaining source groups and independent evaluation.\n\nUse paired source-cluster confidence intervals; report negative results, failed quality gates and detector disagreement. Do not turn detection transfer, geometric orthogonality or lower detector score alone into a claim of universal causal AI-ness. [planning.md](planning.md) specifies the full controls, split logic, intervention scaling, stopping rules and pruned directions.\n\nFinal integrity audit: run `.venv/bin/python scripts/audit_resources.py`; results are recorded in [final_audit.json](artifacts/resource_finder/final_audit.json). This checks local resource integrity and documentation, not scientific outcomes.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
