{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_2","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"pwd; cat planning.md; cat pyproject.toml; cat datasets/README.md; cat code/README.md; rg --files -g AGENTS.md -g '*SKILL*' .codex; cat .codex/skills/data-analysis/SKILL.md\"","aggregated_output":"/workspaces/is-there-a-sounds-like-ai-direction-c05a-codex-gpt-6-astra\n# Experiment handoff: three directions only\n\n## Scope and ranking (2026-10-04)\nQuestion: can a direction estimated from human/AI text representations change the style of **new generations**, with independent detection and content/coherence preserved? “AI-ness” is an operational style construct, not proof of authorship.\n\nScores are judgmental 1–5 (higher better), equally weighted: literature evidence (E), hypothesis relevance (R), expected information gain (I), feasibility (F). Evidence: downloaded 2608.24780 §§4–5 and limitations; 2606.07313 §§3–5 and Appendix F; 2503.03601 §3 and Appendix H; 2601.10387 §§3,5,8; CAA 2312.06681. These scores rank experiments, not confidence that the hypothesis is true.\n\n|Rank|Direction|E|R|I|F|Total|Decision and evidence|\n|---|---|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|---|\n|1|Matched human–AI direction: signed causal generation intervention|5|5|5|5|20|KEEP. Detection is well supported; linear-probe papers do not test generation. CAA supplies intervention recipe.|\n|2|Separate AI-style from formality, length, domain and assistant persona|5|5|5|4|19|KEEP. SAE style confounds and assistant-axis causality make this decisive for distinctness.|\n|3|Cross-domain, cross-generator and second-model transfer of the same causal test|5|5|4|4|18|KEEP. RAID and SV-Detect show transfer failures especially non-chat models.|\n|4|Standalone SAE feature discovery/steering atlas|5|3|3|2|13|PRUNE. Existing 2025 work already steers interpretable detector features; scope/SAE costs high. Use findings only for nuisance choices.|\n|5|Optimize detector evasion or adversarial paraphrasing|4|2|2|4|12|PRUNE. Detector exploitation is not evidence for a causal, content-preserving style direction.|\n|6|Fine-tune a model to write human-like prose|4|2|2|3|11|PRUNE. Changes weights; does not isolate residual direction causality.|\n|7|Full circuits/SAE training or neuron localization|3|3|4|1|11|PRUNE. Expensive, premature before a reliable causal effect.|\n|8|Multilingual universal axis and cross-architecture vector transport|3|3|4|1|11|PRUNE. Broader claim needs alignment between coordinate systems and many controls.|\n\nDo not expand beyond these three unless new evidence invalidates this ranking; document any reranking in the resource_finder notes of STATE.md.\n\n## Shared protocol\n1. Begin with an accessible 1.7–4B instruction model; Qwen3-1.7B is supported by SV-Detect backbone ablations. Pin revision and disable thinking where appropriate. Gemma-2-2B is a useful alternative if weights are accessible; its published SAE results do not transfer feature indices to another model. Inspect GPU before choosing dtype/batch size. Model weights are not part of this resource-gathering deliverable.\n2. Use HAP-E human chunk 2 versus model continuation, joined by document ID; chunk 1 is generation context only. HC3 same-question answers provide second genre/task. Neither corpus guarantees identical propositions across human/AI answers: pair matching controls prompt/topic, not content. Keep raw and cleaned versions. Remove explicit author/model/template identifiers by a fixed rule; record rather than silently drop refusals.\n3. Split by original source document/question **before** tokenization or fitting directions; keep every generator, continuation and attack variant of a source in the same fold. Detect duplicate/near-duplicate material across HC3 and MAGE (MAGE incorporates existing corpora); cross-corpus labels do not guarantee independence. Fit PCA, normalization, nuisance bases, directions and detector thresholds on training/calibration only. Freeze layer/scale selection on validation before final test.\n4. Extract response-only, attention-mask-aware residuals, retaining last-real-token and mean-token alternatives. Record hook location (post-block/post-MLP and pre-final-norm), layer index convention, template and tokenizer. Never pool padding or prompt tokens unintentionally. Probe AUROC does not select the most causal layer by itself.\n5. Estimate AI-minus-human difference of means and L2 logistic normals at each prespecified layer. If using reduced/standardized features, map the weight back to original residual coordinates (PCA transpose and inverse scaling); bias is not a steering vector. Orient sign with training label means. All-layer detector score is not a single physical residual-stream vector.\n\n## Direction 1: causal dose response\nPilot on ~128 held-out prompts, then aim for >=300 independent source prompts, 3 generation seeds. Use benign fact-constrained explanations and source-grounded rewrites, supplemented by HAP-E continuations. Hold prompts, decoding, max tokens and seed pairs constant across conditions. For HAP-E continuation content is not fixed, so analyze separately.\n\nChoose a small middle/late layer grid on validation. Normalize v to unit norm and scale additions in training projection-SD units: h' = h + alpha * sd(<h,v>) * v, alpha in {-2,-1,-0.5,0,0.5,1,2}. Record relative residual norm as well; stop scales yielding pilot collapse by a prespecified coherence criterion. Apply to generated tokens; explicitly define whether the final prompt state predicting the first token is intervened on and ensure cached decoding matches that choice. Compare fixed negative addition with projection removal h' = h - <h,v>v; those are different interventions. Include zero, shuffled-label directions, >=5 norm-matched random directions, and style-prompt baselines.\n\nPrimary outcome: paired change in frozen independent detector score on generated text, excluding prompt. Use a separately trained encoder detector and a different-family likelihood/curvature detector (Fast-DetectGPT) with held-out calibration. Freeze them before steering selection; do not tune against the final detector. If detectors disagree, report disagreement. Do not equate a lower detector score with actual human authorship.\n\nJoint outcomes: factual proposition retention/QA accuracy against supplied source, blinded coherence and instruction-following ratings, repetition and truncation/EOS, and output token counts. Embedding similarity and perplexity are supporting metrics only. Predeclare noninferiority margins after pilot (e.g. <=5 percentage-point factual-accuracy loss, <=0.25 loss on a 1–5 coherence rubric), then freeze before test. Report the full style-versus-quality curve and the fraction meeting BOTH constraints; never select only fluent successes post hoc.\n\n## Direction 2: distinctness and nuisance controls\nBuild matched prompts/texts for formal–informal, concise–verbose, assistant–nonassistant, and domain contrasts with content held fixed as far as possible. Use multiple nuisance prompts and independent source groups. Estimate all nuisance directions in the SAME model/layer coordinate system. Compare cosine alignment and a joint orthonormal nuisance basis Q from training data; v_resid = (I - QQ^T)v. Report removed norm. If the residual norm is negligible, do not normalize numerical noise.\n\nCompare original, residualized, each nuisance-only direction and a norm-matched random residualized direction with identical dose and quality gates. Match text length within domain at training; report generation outcomes in length strata but do not rely solely on post-treatment length regression (length may mediate treatment). A 2x2 crossed style/persona prompt design tests whether the effect persists beyond assistant framing. Geometry alone neither proves nor refutes causal independence; partialling out nuisance directions may remove true style signal too.\n\n## Direction 3: transfer\nTrain directions on selected HAP-E generators/domains; test held-out source groups, domains and generators; HC3/MAGE/RAID provide additional shifts. RAID human rows must be retained and attack variants linked by source_id/adv_source_id. Analyze chat/base sources separately and clean/attacked text separately. Estimate direction independently in a second model family: raw vectors cannot simply be copied across model coordinates. Reuse the same frozen external detectors and joint quality criteria. A detection transfer result alone is not a causal transfer result.\n\n## Analysis and decision rules\nUnit of inference is source prompt/document. Cluster-bootstrap paired differences by source, with all seeds and conditions inside each cluster. Report 95% intervals, domain effects, dose monotonicity and detector agreement. Preregister primary layer/scale comparison after validation; correct or clearly label exploratory layer/dose comparisons. Random-direction controls must share norm and hook. A null result is informative: a readable direction need not control generation. Claim distinct causal AI-style only if signed response survives independent detectors, source/content/coherence controls and nuisance interventions. Otherwise report the narrower result (readout-only, nuisance-mediated, detector-specific, or quality-damaging).\n[project]\nname = \"research-workspace\"\nversion = \"0.1.0\"\ndescription = \"Research workspace for experiments\"\nrequires-python = \">=3.10\"\ndependencies = [\n    \"httpx>=0.28.1\",\n    \"huggingface-hub>=2.1.1\",\n    \"pyarrow>=25.0.1\",\n    \"pymupdf>=1.28.2\",\n    \"pypdf>=6.19.0\",\n    \"requests>=2.34.2\",\n]\n\n[build-system]\nrequires = [\"hatchling\"]\nbuild-backend = \"hatchling.build\"\n\n[tool.uv]\npackage = false\n# Downloaded datasets\n\nAll four requested datasets are locally available. Downloaded raw files are excluded from Git; small **truncated excerpts** are in `samples/`. Exact source revisions, byte counts and SHA256 checksums are in `../artifacts/resource_finder/dataset_downloads.json`. Full-file parsing and schema/count/missingness checks are in `../artifacts/resource_finder/dataset_validation.json`.\n\n## Reproduce downloads and validation\n\nRun from the repository root (contains `.git`):\n\n```bash\npwd\nuv sync\nsource .venv/bin/activate\npython scripts/download_datasets.py\npython scripts/validate_datasets.py\n```\n\nThe downloader pins the three Hugging Face repositories to recorded revisions, checks expected byte lengths, downloads to `.part`, and renames only complete downloads. RAID uses the authors' documented clean-data mirror. Existing files are reused; compare their hashes to the download manifest when reproducing. Metadata snapshots required by the script are tracked under `artifacts/resource_finder/`.\n\n## HC3 — complete English release\n\n- Source: [Hello-SimpleAI/HC3](https://huggingface.co/datasets/Hello-SimpleAI/HC3), revision `4d0ff18143b5a7e1b1e79beb540c04549d1e59d3`.\n- Local: `hc3/all.jsonl`, 73,722,850 bytes; 24,322 question records, 58,546 human answers and 26,903 ChatGPT answers. Five domains: reddit_eli5 17,112; finance 3,933; medicine 1,248; open_qa 1,187; wiki_csai 842.\n- Schema: question, human_answers (list), chatgpt_answers (list), index, source. No official split files in this download; make source-group splits. The specified repository is the English release; this is not the separate Chinese corpus.\n- License: CC-BY-SA-4.0, **subject to stricter original-source terms**, as stated in the card.\n- Validation: 455 empty ChatGPT answer lists; no empty human lists/questions. Only 23,492 unique whitespace-normalized questions and 2,522 unique index values. **Index alone is not a unique key.** Group duplicates by normalized question, retain source and original row ordinal. Do not fabricate missing AI answers. Multiple answers to a question must stay together.\n- Purpose: same-question human/AI comparison, second-task transfer. Topic matched does not mean fact-equivalent.\n\nDirect download:\n\n```bash\npwd\ncurl -L --fail 'https://huggingface.co/datasets/Hello-SimpleAI/HC3/resolve/4d0ff18143b5a7e1b1e79beb540c04549d1e59d3/all.jsonl' -o datasets/hc3/all.jsonl\n```\n\nLoad:\n\n```python\nimport json\nwith open('datasets/hc3/all.jsonl') as f:\n    rows = [json.loads(line) for line in f]\n# Project convention: individual human answer label=0; ChatGPT answer label=1.\n```\n\n## HAP-E — complete requested parallel corpus\n\n- Source: [browndw/human-ai-parallel-corpus](https://huggingface.co/datasets/browndw/human-ai-parallel-corpus), revision `b514ff64988d9e322fd81c5d70d69a38e78491f5`.\n- Local: `hape/*.parquet`, all eight files, 114,086,679 bytes total. Each file has 8,290 records; 66,320 chunks total: two human chunks and six LLM continuations.\n- Schema: `doc_id`, `text`; no nulls or empty texts. All eight files share exactly 8,290 source IDs after stripping `@...`.\n- Domains: acad 1,227; blog 1,526; fic 1,395; news 1,322; spok 1,721; tvm 1,099.\n- Generators: GPT-4o, GPT-4o-mini, Llama3 8B/70B base and instruct. This is the requested original HAP-E, not HAP-E2 or the mini variant.\n- **Use human chunk 2 as human comparator. Human chunk 1 is the prompt context**, not an independent human example. `doc_id.split('@')[0]` is the cross-file join/group key; prefix before `_` encodes domain. No official train/validation/test partitions; split by source ID before pairing.\n- License card: MIT; retain attribution and investigate underlying text-source terms for redistribution. Original corpus filters refusals/incomplete outputs, so it has selection bias. Matched continuations may differ in facts.\n\nDownload all exact files with `scripts/download_datasets.py`; alternatively:\n\n```python\nfrom huggingface_hub import snapshot_download\nsnapshot_download('browndw/human-ai-parallel-corpus', repo_type='dataset',\n    revision='b514ff64988d9e322fd81c5d70d69a38e78491f5',\n    allow_patterns=['text_data/*.parquet'], local_dir='datasets/hape_hf')\n```\n\nThe alternative preserves a `text_data/` subdirectory; the supplied downloader flattens basenames into `hape/`.\n\n```python\nimport pyarrow.parquet as pq\nhuman = pq.read_table('datasets/hape/hape-text_human-chunk-2.parquet').to_pylist()\ncontext = pq.read_table('datasets/hape/hape-text_human-chunk-1.parquet').to_pylist()\n# Join on doc_id.split('@')[0], never row order alone.\n```\n\n## MAGE — all five released CSVs\n\n- Source: [yaful/MAGE](https://huggingface.co/datasets/yaful/MAGE), revision `342663f0a2b775455c023f5d36a1341ff0ec5402`; Apache-2.0 card.\n- Local: `mage/train.csv` 319,071 rows; `valid.csv` 56,792; `test.csv` 56,819; `test_ood_set_gpt.csv` 1,562; `test_ood_set_gpt_para.csv` 2,362. Total 436,606 rows, 553,566,692 bytes.\n- Schema: text, label, src. **Raw label 1 = human; 0 = machine.** Verified against all `src` human suffixes. Invert for project convention AI=1. CSVs have UTF-8 BOM; load with `utf-8-sig`.\n- All CSVs fully parsed. `src` encodes source corpus/generator/prompt variant; raw CSVs lack reliable original-document IDs. Group reconstruction/near-duplicate auditing is needed before building new splits.\n- Found 52 whitespace-normalized exact text overlaps between validation and test. OOD GPT and paraphrase files share 762 human texts; these are not independent tests. No normalized exact overlap found between main train and valid/test, or between downloaded MAGE and HC3; that does not rule out common sources or near duplicates.\n- Use main training for detector fitting only with group precautions; keep OOD evaluations frozen. Do not treat the 762 reused human controls as separate independent observations.\n\nExact files are downloaded by the supplied script. Single-file example:\n\n```bash\npwd\ncurl -L --fail 'https://huggingface.co/datasets/yaful/MAGE/resolve/342663f0a2b775455c023f5d36a1341ff0ec5402/test.csv' -o datasets/mage/test.csv\n```\n\n```python\nimport csv\nwith open('datasets/mage/test.csv', encoding='utf-8-sig', newline='') as f:\n    rows = list(csv.DictReader(f))\nai_labels = [1-int(r['label']) for r in rows]\n```\n\n## RAID — complete clean training partition (documented subset)\n\n- Requested source: [liamdugan/raid](https://huggingface.co/datasets/liamdugan/raid). HF metadata revision `865cac74188466cb0c3b7574a10204007b57a459`; downloaded equivalent clean partition from the [authors' documented mirror](https://github.com/liamdugan/raid#download-raid).\n- Local: `raid/train_none.csv`, 801,662,741 bytes; 467,985 rows = 13,371 human + 454,614 generated texts, eight English domains, eleven generators plus human; 13,371 source groups. All IDs unique, all generations nonempty, all `attack=none`.\n- This is **not full RAID**: the 11.8GB attacked train, extra languages/code, and unlabeled test partition were not downloaded. Initial causal/transfer tests need clean examples; attacked evaluation can be added from the specified source within direction 3.\n- Fields: id, adv_source_id, source_id, model, decoding, repetition_penalty, attack, domain, title, prompt, generation.\n- Human rows have blank prompt/decoding/repetition_penalty (expected, not corruption). Label AI=0 if model is human, else AI=1. Preserve source_id across all generators/decoding/attacks when splitting; source group is the inference unit. Sampling by raw row massively overweights AI texts; balance labels/domain and do not duplicate human controls as independent samples.\n- License card/repository: MIT; original text sources may carry their own terms.\n\nReproduce the downloaded subset:\n\n```bash\npwd\ncurl -L --fail 'https://dataset.raid-bench.xyz/train_none.csv' -o datasets/raid/train_none.csv\n```\n\nFor complete attacks (11.8GB), when needed:\n\n```python\nfrom huggingface_hub import hf_hub_download\nhf_hub_download('liamdugan/raid', 'train.csv', repo_type='dataset',\n    revision='865cac74188466cb0c3b7574a10204007b57a459', local_dir='datasets/raid_full')\n```\n\n```python\nimport csv\nwith open('datasets/raid/train_none.csv', encoding='utf-8-sig', newline='') as f:\n    for row in csv.DictReader(f):\n        label = int(row['model'] != 'human')\n        group = row['source_id']\n        # Process in batches; do not require loading 802MB CSV into memory.\n```\n\n## Experiment requirements\n\nTarget English benign writing with known provenance, prompt/topic matching, original-source grouping, generator/domain labels, comparable lengths, and held-out human negatives. HAP-E is primary; HC3 gives task shift; MAGE/RAID give detector and source-model stress tests. Keep the three-direction budget in `../planning.md`. All preprocessing, label inversion, truncation and exclusions must be logged. Dataset membership is evidence of provenance in that corpus, not an infallible detector target for new text.\n\n## Auxiliary assistant-persona prompt pack (not a fifth benchmark)\n\n`assistant_axis_prompts/` contains all 519 JSON/JSONL files (2,653,728 bytes) extracted from the pinned `safety-research/assistant-axis` repository's `data/` tree. It includes role/trait instructions, lists and extraction questions, for re-estimating persona and nuisance vectors in the chosen model. Files were parsed successfully; SHA256 manifest: `../artifacts/resource_finder/assistant_prompt_manifest.json`. These are prompts, not human/AI detector training labels or pretrained activation vectors. README declares MIT; the clone has no standalone LICENSE file.\n\nReproduce after restoring the code clone:\n\n```bash\npwd\n.venv/bin/python scripts/clone_resources.py\n.venv/bin/python scripts/extract_assistant_prompts.py\n```\n\nPoint the upstream pipeline's `--roles_dir` to the **absolute** `datasets/assistant_axis_prompts/roles/instructions` directory and `--questions_file` to `datasets/assistant_axis_prompts/extraction_questions.jsonl`. Use benign roles and content-controlled questions for the main style experiment. The full 275-role pipeline and its external judge have not been run; a reduced matched control must be described as an adaptation.\n# Code resources\n\nSix official repositories cloned as shallow, filtered, sparse checkouts. Root source/docs and selected code directories are present; upstream corpora, vectors, model weights, results and leaderboard dumps are excluded. Assistant-axis prompt data is separately staged in `../datasets/assistant_axis_prompts/`. Repo code is ignored by parent Git; reproducibility lives in `../artifacts/resource_finder/code_manifest.json` with full commit IDs and selected directories.\n\n## Restore and inspect\n\nFrom the workspace root:\n\n```bash\npwd\n.venv/bin/python scripts/clone_resources.py\n```\n\nNew clones fetch and detach at the recorded commit if upstream HEAD has moved. Existing clones at another revision cause the script to stop rather than overwrite work. To deliberately restore an existing resource clone, run `git -C code/<name> fetch --depth 1 origin <recorded-commit>`, then `git -C code/<name> checkout --detach <recorded-commit>` and rerun the script. Do not overwrite local edits. Use `git -C` to keep the workspace working directory fixed.\n\n|Repository|Commit|Purpose and local entry points|\n|---|---|---|\n|[MGT probes](https://github.com/gerritq/mgt_probes)|`8875297e41846760e7cd06a7a249f581b9364146`|`mgt_probes/src/probes/probe_main.py`: `LinearProbing._train_linear_probe` (LLP), `_train_meta_probe` (CLP); `src/inference.py` extracts activations. `probe.sh` is the experiment driver.|\n|[SV-Detect](https://github.com/Atmyre/sv-detect)|`d8d6541568c572bc29d53e43e870fd7dfc5d6dbc`|`sv-detect/src/extract/nb_pipeline.py`: `compute_sv`, `dot_products`, `build_detector`; CLI extraction/construction/head modules; `src/interpret/regex_detector*.py` supplies nuisance surface baselines.|\n|[Assistant Axis](https://github.com/safety-research/assistant-axis)|`a98961956072224eaf244eb289d6c01700b63795`|`assistant-axis/assistant_axis/axis.py` computes contrast; `steering.py` has `ActivationSteering` addition/ablation/capping context manager. `pipeline/` five-stage extraction, judge, aggregation workflow; `notebooks/` examples.|\n|[CAA](https://github.com/nrimsky/CAA)|`5dabbbd9a0bca5f25e174501e959de378806aa48`|`CAA/generate_vectors.py`, `normalize_vectors.py`, `llama_wrapper.py`, `prompting_with_steering.py`; `utils/` helpers. Adapt paired difference and hook logic rather than full old environment.|\n|[Fast-DetectGPT](https://github.com/baoguangsheng/fast-detect-gpt)|`971b05202bac2bb504d60c0ac0812fea7a8f7c82`|`fast-detect-gpt/scripts/fast_detect_gpt.py:get_sampling_discrepancy_analytic`; `scripts/local_infer.py:FastDetectGPT.compute_crit`; `scripts/model.py` model loaders. Independent frozen external score.|\n|[RAID](https://github.com/liamdugan/raid)|`f450f4b1e0bfd9dc2e3475d10ec3bd70cc7153f1`|`raid/raid/` package has detection/evaluation; `detect_cli.py`, `evaluate_cli.py`; `detectors/` adapters and `generation/` decoding/attack references. Prefer local clean CSV over default massive downloads.|\n\n## Dependencies and adaptation decisions\n\n**MGT probes.** Python >=3.11,<3.13; upstream pins Torch 2.5.1/CUDA12.1 and has a broad dependency list (Transformers, sklearn, datasets, PEFT, etc.). `BASE_COE` is required for its paths. Code standardizes activations **before PCA**, unlike a naive reading of the shortened paper method; undo scaling and PCA when exporting a residual-space direction. `Inference.run` is single-example, truncates to 1024 and reads `outputs.hidden_states`, whose indexing/final normalization must be reconciled with intervention hooks. No license file identified.\n\n**SV-Detect.** Upstream asks for Python >=3.11, Torch >=2.10/CUDA12.8, Transformers >=4.57, numpy>=2.2, pandas>=3, sklearn>=1.8. This differs from the other repos; do not install all requirements wholesale into one environment. `SVDETECT_BASE` selects artifact root. Source-code caveats at the pinned commit:\n\n- README/YAML give vector logistic `C=0.01`; `nb_pipeline._sv_logreg_layer` uses **C=1.0**, per-pair centering, no intercept. This requires equal-length meaningful pairs; arbitrary row pairing is not a valid paired control.\n- README says head `fit_intercept=False`; `build_detector()` leaves sklearn's default intercept enabled. Decide and record one implementation; no exact-reproduction claim until reconciled.\n- Mean vectors and cosine normalization have no zero-norm guard. PCA signs need orientation with labels. QR requires rank checks if used for nuisance removal.\n- `ActivationRecorder` uses mean over tokens for single unpadded examples; adapt masks before batching or response-only extraction.\n- These scripts detect text; they do not causally steer generation. No license file identified.\n\n**Assistant Axis.** Python>=3.10; Torch>=2, Transformers>=4.40, accelerate, numpy/sklearn, vLLM, plotting/notebooks, judge API dependency. A minimal hook adaptation need not install the entire vLLM/judge stack. `compute_axis` returns an unnormalized assistant-minus-role mean; `ActivationSteering` converts dtype/device but does not silently impose unit norm. `positions='all'` affects prompt positions too if enabled during prefill; explicitly implement the intended generated-token policy. Full pipeline needs generated role responses and a judge; it has not been run. Released vectors are for 27B/32B/70B models and **cannot be reused as vectors for a 1.7B model**. README declares MIT, no standalone license file.\n\n**CAA.** Original Llama2 access and GPT4 judge credentials are needed for full upstream replication. Old pins include Torch2.0.1, Transformers4.33.2, numpy1.24.4 and datasets2.14.5; requirements contain contradictory pytest pins (7.4.2 and 8.2.1). Do not bulk-install on this Python3.12 environment. Core extraction/steering logic is reusable with a compatible current model stack. MIT.\n\n**Fast-DetectGPT.** Requirements pin Transformers4.28.1/datasets2.12.0; README's Python3.8/Torch1.10 environment is historical, while current code includes newer model choices. Adapt the analytic criterion, validate tokenizer alignment for reference/scoring models, and use raw criterion or locally calibrated score. The demo's hardcoded Gaussian “probabilities” are not universal probabilities and can be nonmonotonic in the criterion. Do not use those constants as independent calibrated outcomes. MIT.\n\n**RAID.** Package pins numpy1.26.4/pandas2.2.2/sklearn1.3.2; full detector environment is much larger. `run_detection`, `run_evaluation` and subgroup TPR at calibrated FPR are reusable. Default loader includes attacks; explicitly use the local `datasets/raid/train_none.csv`. Recalibrate on separate human negatives, never on final steered outputs. MIT.\n\n## Validation performed and limits\n\nRead all six READMEs and their dependency files, checked listed entry points and inspected critical extraction/steering/scoring functions. Parsed all **198 checked-out Python files** with Python3.12 AST: no syntax errors; older CAA/RAID regex/LaTeX strings emitted escape-sequence warnings. This is **not a runtime or numerical correctness test**. No generator, detector inference, external judge, upstream benchmark, or model download was run. No experiments are claimed.\n\nAvailable hardware query: one NVIDIA RTX A6000, 49,140 MiB reported total memory. Resource environment contains only download/PDF/data-inspection dependencies, not Torch/Transformers. Experiment runner should install one compatible model stack into the project `.venv` with `uv add`, pin model revisions, and first verify: zero steering reproduces baseline; cached/uncached token policies agree; padding is masked; score sign and PCA inverse map are correct. Follow `../planning.md` rather than launching all upstream scripts.\n.codex/skills/data-analysis/SKILL.md\n.codex/skills/literature-review/SKILL.md\n.codex/skills/paper-writer/SKILL.md\n.codex/skills/research-brainstorming/SKILL.md\n.codex/skills/paper-finder/SKILL.md\n.codex/skills/grant-writing/SKILL.md\n.codex/skills/hypothesis-generation/SKILL.md\n.codex/skills/axiom-lean-prover/SKILL.md\n.codex/skills/critical-analysis/SKILL.md\n.codex/skills/peer-review/SKILL.md\n.codex/skills/citation-manager/SKILL.md\n---\nname: data-analysis\ndescription: Conduct exploratory data analysis and statistical testing with test selection guidance. Use when exploring datasets, selecting statistical tests, performing power analysis, or preparing results for publication.\n---\n\n# Data Analysis\n\nGuidance for exploratory data analysis and statistical testing.\n\n## When to Use\n\n- Exploring new datasets\n- Selecting appropriate statistical tests\n- Performing power analysis\n- Reporting results in papers\n- Validating experimental results\n\n## Exploratory Data Analysis (EDA)\n\n### EDA Workflow\n\n1. **Load and Inspect**: Basic data structure\n2. **Summarize**: Descriptive statistics\n3. **Visualize**: Distributions and relationships\n4. **Identify Issues**: Missing data, outliers\n5. **Document Findings**: Key insights\n\n### Initial Inspection\n\n```python\n# Basic checks\ndf.shape          # Dimensions\ndf.dtypes         # Data types\ndf.head()         # First rows\ndf.describe()     # Summary stats\ndf.isnull().sum() # Missing values\n```\n\n### Key Questions to Answer\n\n| Question | What to Check |\n|----------|---------------|\n| What's the size? | Rows, columns, data types |\n| Any missing data? | Null counts, patterns |\n| What's the distribution? | Histograms, descriptive stats |\n| Any outliers? | Box plots, z-scores |\n| Any relationships? | Correlations, scatter plots |\n| Any patterns? | Trends, clusters, groups |\n\n### Visualization Guide\n\n| Data Type | Visualization |\n|-----------|---------------|\n| Single continuous | Histogram, density plot, box plot |\n| Single categorical | Bar chart, pie chart |\n| Two continuous | Scatter plot, line plot |\n| Two categorical | Grouped bar chart, heatmap |\n| Continuous + categorical | Box plot by group, violin plot |\n| Time series | Line plot with time axis |\n\n## Statistical Test Selection\n\n### Decision Tree\n\n```\nQuestion: What are you trying to do?\n│\n├─ Compare groups\n│   │\n│   ├─ How many groups?\n│   │   ├─ 2 groups → See \"Two Group Comparisons\"\n│   │   └─ 3+ groups → See \"Multiple Group Comparisons\"\n│   │\n│   └─ Related or independent?\n│       ├─ Independent (different subjects)\n│       └─ Related (same subjects, before/after)\n│\n├─ Examine relationships\n│   ├─ Two variables → Correlation, regression\n│   └─ Multiple variables → Multiple regression\n│\n└─ Test proportions\n    └─ Chi-square test\n```\n\n### Two Group Comparisons\n\n| Data Type | Independent Groups | Related Groups |\n|-----------|-------------------|----------------|\n| **Normal** | Independent t-test | Paired t-test |\n| **Non-normal** | Mann-Whitney U | Wilcoxon signed-rank |\n\n### Multiple Group Comparisons\n\n| Data Type | Independent Groups | Related Groups |\n|-----------|-------------------|----------------|\n| **Normal** | One-way ANOVA | Repeated measures ANOVA |\n| **Non-normal** | Kruskal-Wallis | Friedman test |\n\n### Checking Assumptions\n\n**Normality Tests**:\n- Shapiro-Wilk (n < 50)\n- Kolmogorov-Smirnov (n ≥ 50)\n- Visual: Q-Q plot\n\n**Homogeneity of Variance**:\n- Levene's test\n- Visual: Box plots by group\n\n**Independence**:\n- By experimental design\n- Durbin-Watson (for residuals)\n\n### When Assumptions Fail\n\n| Violation | Solution |\n|-----------|----------|\n| Non-normality | Non-parametric test, transformation |\n| Unequal variance | Welch's t-test, transformation |\n| Non-independence | Mixed-effects model |\n| Outliers | Robust methods, removal (with justification) |\n\n## Effect Sizes\n\n### Why Effect Sizes Matter\n\n- p-values tell you if effect exists, not how big\n- Effect sizes quantify the magnitude\n- Required for power analysis\n- Better for meta-analysis\n\n### Common Effect Sizes\n\n| Measure | Context | Interpretation |\n|---------|---------|----------------|\n| **Cohen's d** | Two means | 0.2=small, 0.5=medium, 0.8=large |\n| **Pearson's r** | Correlation | 0.1=small, 0.3=medium, 0.5=large |\n| **Eta-squared** | ANOVA | 0.01=small, 0.06=medium, 0.14=large |\n| **Odds ratio** | Categorical | 1.5=small, 2.5=medium, 4=large |\n\n### Computing Effect Sizes\n\n```python\n# Cohen's d for two groups\nimport numpy as np\n\ndef cohens_d(group1, group2):\n    n1, n2 = len(group1), len(group2)\n    var1, var2 = np.var(group1, ddof=1), np.var(group2, ddof=1)\n    pooled_std = np.sqrt(((n1-1)*var1 + (n2-1)*var2) / (n1+n2-2))\n    return (np.mean(group1) - np.mean(group2)) / pooled_std\n```\n\n## Power Analysis\n\n### Key Concepts\n\n| Term | Definition |\n|------|------------|\n| **Power** | Probability of detecting true effect (1-β) |\n| **α (alpha)** | False positive rate (typically 0.05) |\n| **β (beta)** | False negative rate (typically 0.20) |\n| **Effect size** | Magnitude of effect |\n| **Sample size** | Number of observations |\n\n### Power Analysis Uses\n\n1. **A priori**: Before study, determine needed sample size\n2. **Post hoc**: After study, calculate achieved power\n3. **Sensitivity**: Given n and power, what effect detectable?\n\n### Sample Size Calculation\n\n```python\nfrom statsmodels.stats.power import TTestIndPower\n\nanalysis = TTestIndPower()\n\n# Sample size for t-test\nn = analysis.solve_power(\n    effect_size=0.5,    # Cohen's d\n    alpha=0.05,         # Significance level\n    power=0.80,         # Desired power\n    ratio=1.0,          # n2/n1\n    alternative='two-sided'\n)\n```\n\n### Power Guidelines\n\n| Power | Interpretation |\n|-------|----------------|\n| < 0.50 | Inadequate |\n| 0.50-0.70 | Low |\n| 0.70-0.80 | Moderate |\n| ≥ 0.80 | Adequate (standard target) |\n| ≥ 0.90 | High |\n\n## Multiple Comparisons\n\n### The Problem\n\n- Each test has α chance of false positive\n- Multiple tests inflate false positive rate\n- Family-wise error rate: 1-(1-α)^n\n\n### Correction Methods\n\n| Method | When to Use | Strictness |\n|--------|-------------|------------|\n| **Bonferroni** | Few comparisons | Most conservative |\n| **Holm** | Few comparisons | Less conservative |\n| **Benjamini-Hochberg** | Many comparisons | Controls FDR |\n| **Tukey HSD** | Post-hoc ANOVA | Common choice |\n\n### Applying Corrections\n\n```python\nfrom scipy import stats\nimport numpy as np\n\n# Bonferroni\nadjusted_alpha = 0.05 / num_tests\n\n# Holm-Bonferroni\nfrom statsmodels.stats.multitest import multipletests\nreject, pvals_corrected, _, _ = multipletests(pvals, method='holm')\n\n# Benjamini-Hochberg (FDR)\nreject, pvals_corrected, _, _ = multipletests(pvals, method='fdr_bh')\n```\n\n## Reporting Results\n\n### APA Format\n\n**t-test**:\n```\nt(df) = X.XX, p = .XXX, d = X.XX\n```\nExample: t(45) = 2.34, p = .023, d = 0.68\n\n**ANOVA**:\n```\nF(df1, df2) = X.XX, p = .XXX, η² = .XX\n```\nExample: F(2, 87) = 4.56, p = .013, η² = .095\n\n**Correlation**:\n```\nr(df) = .XX, p = .XXX\n```\nExample: r(48) = .42, p = .003\n\n**Chi-square**:\n```\nχ²(df, N = n) = X.XX, p = .XXX\n```\nExample: χ²(2, N = 150) = 6.78, p = .034\n\n### Reporting Guidelines\n\n**Do**:\n- Report exact p-values (not just < .05)\n- Include effect sizes\n- Report confidence intervals\n- Describe what was tested\n\n**Don't**:\n- Say \"proved\" or \"significant difference\" alone\n- Report only significant results\n- Cherry-pick tests\n- Over-interpret p = .049 vs p = .051\n\n### Results Table Format\n\n```\nTable 1: Comparison of Methods on Benchmark X\n\nMethod      Mean (SD)       95% CI          Effect Size\n------------------------------------------------------\nBaseline    75.2 (3.4)     [73.1, 77.3]    -\nMethod A    78.9 (2.8)*    [77.2, 80.6]    d = 0.52\nMethod B    81.4 (3.1)**   [79.5, 83.3]    d = 0.89\n\nNote: * p < .05, ** p < .01 vs Baseline\n```\n\n## Common Pitfalls\n\n| Pitfall | Problem | Solution |\n|---------|---------|----------|\n| P-hacking | Running tests until significant | Pre-register analysis |\n| HARKing | Hypothesizing after results known | State hypotheses before |\n| Multiple comparisons | Inflated false positives | Apply correction |\n| Pseudo-replication | Non-independent samples | Mixed-effects models |\n| Ignoring effect sizes | Significant ≠ important | Report effect sizes |\n\n## Quality Checklist\n\n- [ ] Research questions clearly stated\n- [ ] Appropriate test selected and justified\n- [ ] Assumptions checked\n- [ ] Sample size justified (power analysis)\n- [ ] Multiple comparisons corrected\n- [ ] Effect sizes reported\n- [ ] Confidence intervals included\n- [ ] Results correctly interpreted\n- [ ] Limitations acknowledged\n\n## References\n\nSee `references/` folder for:\n- `test_selection.md`: Detailed test selection guide\n- `apa_reporting.md`: Complete APA reporting templates\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
