{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_5","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"pwd; source .venv/bin/activate; uv add torch 'transformers>=4.51,<5' 'huggingface-hub<1' numpy pandas scipy scikit-learn matplotlib seaborn accelerate sentencepiece; cat .codex/skills/data-analysis/SKILL.md; rg --files datasets/hape; rg --files code/CAA | head; rg --files code/assistant-axis | head\"","aggregated_output":"/workspaces/is-there-a-sounds-like-ai-direction-c05a-codex-gpt-6-astra\nResolved 88 packages in 1.50s\nPrepared 52 packages in 28.80s\nUninstalled 1 package in 40ms\nwarning: Failed to hardlink files; falling back to full copy. This may lead to degraded performance.\n         If the cache and target directories are on different filesystems, hardlinking may not be supported.\n         If this is intentional, set `export UV_LINK_MODE=copy` or use `--link-mode=copy` to suppress this warning.\nInstalled 52 packages in 23.88s\nBytecode compiled 12754 files in 3.42s\n + accelerate==1.15.0\n + cloudpickle==3.1.2\n + contourpy==1.4.0\n + cuda-bindings==13.4.3\n + cuda-pathfinder==1.8.3\n + cuda-toolkit==13.0.3.0\n + cycler==0.12.1\n + fonttools==4.66.1\n - huggingface-hub==2.1.1\n + huggingface-hub==0.36.2\n + jinja2==3.1.6\n + joblib==1.6.0\n + kiwisolver==1.5.1\n + markupsafe==3.0.4\n + matplotlib==3.11.2\n + mpmath==1.3.0\n + narwhals==2.26.0\n + networkx==3.7\n + numpy==2.5.3\n + nvidia-cublas==13.1.1.3\n + nvidia-cuda-cupti==13.0.85\n + nvidia-cuda-nvrtc==13.0.88\n + nvidia-cuda-runtime==13.0.96\n + nvidia-cudnn-cu13==9.24.0.43\n + nvidia-cufft==12.0.0.61\n + nvidia-cufile==1.15.1.6\n + nvidia-curand==10.4.0.35\n + nvidia-cusolver==12.0.4.66\n + nvidia-cusparse==12.6.3.3\n + nvidia-cusparselt-cu13==0.8.1\n + nvidia-nccl-cu13==2.30.7\n + nvidia-nvjitlink==13.4.92\n + nvidia-nvshmem-cu13==3.4.5\n + nvidia-nvtx==13.0.85\n + pandas==3.0.6\n + pillow==12.3.0\n + psutil==7.2.2\n + pyparsing==3.3.3\n + python-dateutil==2.9.0.post0\n + regex==2026.9.29\n + safetensors==0.8.0\n + scikit-learn==1.9.1\n + scipy==1.18.1\n + seaborn==0.13.2\n + sentencepiece==0.2.2\n + setuptools==84.0.0\n + six==1.17.0\n + sympy==1.14.0\n + threadpoolctl==3.7.0\n + tokenizers==0.22.2\n + torch==2.14.1\n + transformers==4.57.6\n + triton==3.8.0\n---\nname: data-analysis\ndescription: Conduct exploratory data analysis and statistical testing with test selection guidance. Use when exploring datasets, selecting statistical tests, performing power analysis, or preparing results for publication.\n---\n\n# Data Analysis\n\nGuidance for exploratory data analysis and statistical testing.\n\n## When to Use\n\n- Exploring new datasets\n- Selecting appropriate statistical tests\n- Performing power analysis\n- Reporting results in papers\n- Validating experimental results\n\n## Exploratory Data Analysis (EDA)\n\n### EDA Workflow\n\n1. **Load and Inspect**: Basic data structure\n2. **Summarize**: Descriptive statistics\n3. **Visualize**: Distributions and relationships\n4. **Identify Issues**: Missing data, outliers\n5. **Document Findings**: Key insights\n\n### Initial Inspection\n\n```python\n# Basic checks\ndf.shape          # Dimensions\ndf.dtypes         # Data types\ndf.head()         # First rows\ndf.describe()     # Summary stats\ndf.isnull().sum() # Missing values\n```\n\n### Key Questions to Answer\n\n| Question | What to Check |\n|----------|---------------|\n| What's the size? | Rows, columns, data types |\n| Any missing data? | Null counts, patterns |\n| What's the distribution? | Histograms, descriptive stats |\n| Any outliers? | Box plots, z-scores |\n| Any relationships? | Correlations, scatter plots |\n| Any patterns? | Trends, clusters, groups |\n\n### Visualization Guide\n\n| Data Type | Visualization |\n|-----------|---------------|\n| Single continuous | Histogram, density plot, box plot |\n| Single categorical | Bar chart, pie chart |\n| Two continuous | Scatter plot, line plot |\n| Two categorical | Grouped bar chart, heatmap |\n| Continuous + categorical | Box plot by group, violin plot |\n| Time series | Line plot with time axis |\n\n## Statistical Test Selection\n\n### Decision Tree\n\n```\nQuestion: What are you trying to do?\n│\n├─ Compare groups\n│   │\n│   ├─ How many groups?\n│   │   ├─ 2 groups → See \"Two Group Comparisons\"\n│   │   └─ 3+ groups → See \"Multiple Group Comparisons\"\n│   │\n│   └─ Related or independent?\n│       ├─ Independent (different subjects)\n│       └─ Related (same subjects, before/after)\n│\n├─ Examine relationships\n│   ├─ Two variables → Correlation, regression\n│   └─ Multiple variables → Multiple regression\n│\n└─ Test proportions\n    └─ Chi-square test\n```\n\n### Two Group Comparisons\n\n| Data Type | Independent Groups | Related Groups |\n|-----------|-------------------|----------------|\n| **Normal** | Independent t-test | Paired t-test |\n| **Non-normal** | Mann-Whitney U | Wilcoxon signed-rank |\n\n### Multiple Group Comparisons\n\n| Data Type | Independent Groups | Related Groups |\n|-----------|-------------------|----------------|\n| **Normal** | One-way ANOVA | Repeated measures ANOVA |\n| **Non-normal** | Kruskal-Wallis | Friedman test |\n\n### Checking Assumptions\n\n**Normality Tests**:\n- Shapiro-Wilk (n < 50)\n- Kolmogorov-Smirnov (n ≥ 50)\n- Visual: Q-Q plot\n\n**Homogeneity of Variance**:\n- Levene's test\n- Visual: Box plots by group\n\n**Independence**:\n- By experimental design\n- Durbin-Watson (for residuals)\n\n### When Assumptions Fail\n\n| Violation | Solution |\n|-----------|----------|\n| Non-normality | Non-parametric test, transformation |\n| Unequal variance | Welch's t-test, transformation |\n| Non-independence | Mixed-effects model |\n| Outliers | Robust methods, removal (with justification) |\n\n## Effect Sizes\n\n### Why Effect Sizes Matter\n\n- p-values tell you if effect exists, not how big\n- Effect sizes quantify the magnitude\n- Required for power analysis\n- Better for meta-analysis\n\n### Common Effect Sizes\n\n| Measure | Context | Interpretation |\n|---------|---------|----------------|\n| **Cohen's d** | Two means | 0.2=small, 0.5=medium, 0.8=large |\n| **Pearson's r** | Correlation | 0.1=small, 0.3=medium, 0.5=large |\n| **Eta-squared** | ANOVA | 0.01=small, 0.06=medium, 0.14=large |\n| **Odds ratio** | Categorical | 1.5=small, 2.5=medium, 4=large |\n\n### Computing Effect Sizes\n\n```python\n# Cohen's d for two groups\nimport numpy as np\n\ndef cohens_d(group1, group2):\n    n1, n2 = len(group1), len(group2)\n    var1, var2 = np.var(group1, ddof=1), np.var(group2, ddof=1)\n    pooled_std = np.sqrt(((n1-1)*var1 + (n2-1)*var2) / (n1+n2-2))\n    return (np.mean(group1) - np.mean(group2)) / pooled_std\n```\n\n## Power Analysis\n\n### Key Concepts\n\n| Term | Definition |\n|------|------------|\n| **Power** | Probability of detecting true effect (1-β) |\n| **α (alpha)** | False positive rate (typically 0.05) |\n| **β (beta)** | False negative rate (typically 0.20) |\n| **Effect size** | Magnitude of effect |\n| **Sample size** | Number of observations |\n\n### Power Analysis Uses\n\n1. **A priori**: Before study, determine needed sample size\n2. **Post hoc**: After study, calculate achieved power\n3. **Sensitivity**: Given n and power, what effect detectable?\n\n### Sample Size Calculation\n\n```python\nfrom statsmodels.stats.power import TTestIndPower\n\nanalysis = TTestIndPower()\n\n# Sample size for t-test\nn = analysis.solve_power(\n    effect_size=0.5,    # Cohen's d\n    alpha=0.05,         # Significance level\n    power=0.80,         # Desired power\n    ratio=1.0,          # n2/n1\n    alternative='two-sided'\n)\n```\n\n### Power Guidelines\n\n| Power | Interpretation |\n|-------|----------------|\n| < 0.50 | Inadequate |\n| 0.50-0.70 | Low |\n| 0.70-0.80 | Moderate |\n| ≥ 0.80 | Adequate (standard target) |\n| ≥ 0.90 | High |\n\n## Multiple Comparisons\n\n### The Problem\n\n- Each test has α chance of false positive\n- Multiple tests inflate false positive rate\n- Family-wise error rate: 1-(1-α)^n\n\n### Correction Methods\n\n| Method | When to Use | Strictness |\n|--------|-------------|------------|\n| **Bonferroni** | Few comparisons | Most conservative |\n| **Holm** | Few comparisons | Less conservative |\n| **Benjamini-Hochberg** | Many comparisons | Controls FDR |\n| **Tukey HSD** | Post-hoc ANOVA | Common choice |\n\n### Applying Corrections\n\n```python\nfrom scipy import stats\nimport numpy as np\n\n# Bonferroni\nadjusted_alpha = 0.05 / num_tests\n\n# Holm-Bonferroni\nfrom statsmodels.stats.multitest import multipletests\nreject, pvals_corrected, _, _ = multipletests(pvals, method='holm')\n\n# Benjamini-Hochberg (FDR)\nreject, pvals_corrected, _, _ = multipletests(pvals, method='fdr_bh')\n```\n\n## Reporting Results\n\n### APA Format\n\n**t-test**:\n```\nt(df) = X.XX, p = .XXX, d = X.XX\n```\nExample: t(45) = 2.34, p = .023, d = 0.68\n\n**ANOVA**:\n```\nF(df1, df2) = X.XX, p = .XXX, η² = .XX\n```\nExample: F(2, 87) = 4.56, p = .013, η² = .095\n\n**Correlation**:\n```\nr(df) = .XX, p = .XXX\n```\nExample: r(48) = .42, p = .003\n\n**Chi-square**:\n```\nχ²(df, N = n) = X.XX, p = .XXX\n```\nExample: χ²(2, N = 150) = 6.78, p = .034\n\n### Reporting Guidelines\n\n**Do**:\n- Report exact p-values (not just < .05)\n- Include effect sizes\n- Report confidence intervals\n- Describe what was tested\n\n**Don't**:\n- Say \"proved\" or \"significant difference\" alone\n- Report only significant results\n- Cherry-pick tests\n- Over-interpret p = .049 vs p = .051\n\n### Results Table Format\n\n```\nTable 1: Comparison of Methods on Benchmark X\n\nMethod      Mean (SD)       95% CI          Effect Size\n------------------------------------------------------\nBaseline    75.2 (3.4)     [73.1, 77.3]    -\nMethod A    78.9 (2.8)*    [77.2, 80.6]    d = 0.52\nMethod B    81.4 (3.1)**   [79.5, 83.3]    d = 0.89\n\nNote: * p < .05, ** p < .01 vs Baseline\n```\n\n## Common Pitfalls\n\n| Pitfall | Problem | Solution |\n|---------|---------|----------|\n| P-hacking | Running tests until significant | Pre-register analysis |\n| HARKing | Hypothesizing after results known | State hypotheses before |\n| Multiple comparisons | Inflated false positives | Apply correction |\n| Pseudo-replication | Non-independent samples | Mixed-effects models |\n| Ignoring effect sizes | Significant ≠ important | Report effect sizes |\n\n## Quality Checklist\n\n- [ ] Research questions clearly stated\n- [ ] Appropriate test selected and justified\n- [ ] Assumptions checked\n- [ ] Sample size justified (power analysis)\n- [ ] Multiple comparisons corrected\n- [ ] Effect sizes reported\n- [ ] Confidence intervals included\n- [ ] Results correctly interpreted\n- [ ] Limitations acknowledged\n\n## References\n\nSee `references/` folder for:\n- `test_selection.md`: Detailed test selection guide\n- `apa_reporting.md`: Complete APA reporting templates\ncode/CAA/prompting_with_steering.py\ncode/CAA/LICENSE\ncode/CAA/scoring.py\ncode/CAA/behaviors.py\ncode/CAA/requirements.txt\ncode/CAA/steering_settings.py\ncode/CAA/activation_steering_interp.ipynb\ncode/CAA/README.md\ncode/CAA/generate_vectors.py\ncode/CAA/plot_results.py\ncode/assistant-axis/README.md\ncode/assistant-axis/assistant_axis/models.py\ncode/assistant-axis/assistant_axis/axis.py\ncode/assistant-axis/notebooks/visualize_axis.ipynb\ncode/assistant-axis/notebooks/pca.ipynb\ncode/assistant-axis/notebooks/project_transcipt.ipynb\ncode/assistant-axis/notebooks/steer.ipynb\ncode/assistant-axis/notebooks/README.md\ncode/assistant-axis/assistant_axis/pca.py\ncode/assistant-axis/assistant_axis/steering.py\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_8","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"pwd; source .venv/bin/activate; python - <<'PY'\nfrom pathlib import Path\np=Path('planning.md')\ns=p.read_text().replace('document any reranking in the resource_finder notes of STATE.md','document any reranking in the experiment_runner notes of STATE.md')\ns+='''\n\n## Motivation & Novelty Assessment (execution preregistration)\n### Why This Research Matters\nA causal writing-style direction would distinguish readable authorship information from controllable generation behavior. This matters for interpreting detectors and separating surface style from assistant identity.\n### Gap in Existing Work\nThe reviewed probes establish readout, while SAE work already changes individual stylistic features, sometimes corrupting facts. A single direction with independent style measurement and preserved content is not established.\n### Our Novel Contribution\nTest the same estimated residual normal under signed additions, ablation, nuisance removal, random controls and independent scoring; quantify base/instruct differences without assuming universal AI authorship semantics.\n### Experiment Justification\n1. Causal signed interventions test whether reading predicts writing control.\n2. Formality/persona/verbosity controls test alternative mechanisms.\n3. Held-out generators/domains, HC3 and a paired base checkpoint test transfer and tuning effects.\n\n## Frozen feasible execution protocol\nThe original handoff is aspirational; this preregistration fixes the session-sized experiment before outcomes. Use Qwen2.5-1.5B-Instruct and Qwen2.5-1.5B (historical paired checkpoints, chosen for accessible internals, aligned architectures and practical repeated intervention; not contemporary capability rankings). One RTX A6000, bfloat16 inference, batch 32 (activation extraction) / 16 (generation), max input 192 tokens and max output 160 tokens. Install only the workspace venv. Live OpenRouter catalog is archived before selection; independent current judge google/gemini-3.8-flash, temperature 0, JSON ratings, default routing. Frozen external encoder Hello-SimpleAI/chatgpt-detector-roberta (older detector, domain shift explicitly tested). These are independent of fit activations, but their errors need not be independent.\n\nHAP-E: 384 training, 96 validation, 128 test source documents from acad/blog/news; 128 OOD documents from fic/spok/tvm. Train on GPT-4o continuations vs human chunk2, test on GPT-4o and held-out Llama3 base/instruct. Restrict each paired text to first 128 whitespace words before tokenizer truncation. Remove exact normalized duplicate texts across folds. HC3: 128 question pairs, no fitting. Last-real-token post-block residuals at layers 8, 16, 24 (one-based); select layer by validation mean-difference AUROC, never generation detector performance. Logistic normal secondary. All vectors unit normalized; dose in training projection SD of the mean direction. Add at last prompt token and each cached generated token, leaving earlier prompt states untouched. Ablate the same states with raw projection removal. Equal-norm random/formality/persona/verbosity/residualized controls use the same absolute addition norm.\n\n64 held-out fact-constrained writing prompts across 8 benign topics, 8 varied factual records each. These are procedurally assembled source records, not simulated model responses. Original outputs always from real checkpoint inference. 64 independent records are the inferential unit; shared templates constrain external generalization. Conditions: zero; mean direction ±1, ±2 SD; logistic -2; ablation; five random -2 directions; formality -2; assistant -2; residualized ±2; human-style prompt. Seed 42 all; seed 43 zero and mean ±2; base checkpoint zero and mean ±2 seed42. Greedy nuisance-pair generations from separate training tasks estimate formal-minus-casual, verbose-minus-concise, assistant-minus-fictional-narrator vectors. These are reduced proxies, not replication of the published Assistant Axis. Domain and length vectors estimated from training residuals; jointly remove all five nuisance directions. Log geometry and projection correlations with length and model NLL.\n\nPrimary comparison: instruct mean -2 vs zero, separately on encoder and blinded judge AI-likeness. Paired bootstrap by prompt (5000 resamples), sign-flip permutation (10000), Holm across the two primary detector tests; exploratory contrasts labeled. Report paired dz and all conditions. Coherence noninferiority margin 0.25 on 1–5; source-fact retention margin 0.05; 95% lower confidence limits must exceed negative margins for preservation. Also compare common coherence strata descriptively (post-treatment conditioning is not causal identification). Judge rates content coverage 0–1 and coherence 1–5; deterministic anchor recall is supplementary. Validate detectors on known human/AI held-out texts. No claim of universal direction from detector-only changes. At n64, approximate 80% detectable paired standardized effect ~0.35 without multiplicity; smaller effects remain uncertain.\n\nMilestones: setup/design 15–25min; extraction/nuisance/generation 30–90min; scoring 15–45min; analysis/report/validation 20–40min, with 25% debugging buffer. Save all raw outputs, revisions, vectors and metrics. Validate hook arithmetic, zero-hook equivalence, one repeated generation batch and deterministic analysis rerun. Failure contingencies: reduce batch on OOM, retain failed API requests and retry, document unavailable measures. No detector-guided vector optimization. No additional research directions.\n'''\np.write_text(s)\np=Path('STATE.md');s=p.read_text();a='<\"'!-- NEURICO_AGENT_NOTES_START:experiment_runner -->'\"';b='<\"'!-- NEURICO_AGENT_NOTES_END:experiment_runner -->'\"';s=s[:s.index(a)+len(a)]+'\\\\nPhases 0–1 complete: resources reviewed; motivation, three-direction ranking and frozen execution protocol in planning.md. A6000 49,140 MiB available, existing isolated .venv activated. Live catalog archived results/live_catalog.json. Reduced paired Qwen2.5-1.5B checkpoints chosen for mechanism/base-instruct comparison; independent encoder plus current Gemini judge. Next Phase 2: install stack, prepare grouped data, pin weights; then implement and run. Scope limits: n64 prompts, reduced persona proxy, historical small checkpoint.\\\\n'+s[s.index(b):];p.write_text(s)\nPath('src').mkdir(exist_ok=True);Path('figures').mkdir(exist_ok=True)\nPY\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
